/mesh/D010284|http://identifiers.org/snomedct/51254007|http://www.ebi.ac.uk/efo/EFO_0007420\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|otar|rare\n13011: MONDO:0018628\tbiolink:Disease\tHIV-associated cancer\t\tGARD:0021857|MEDGEN:1843219|NCIT:C4046|Orphanet:443291|UMLS:C5680059\tmondo-rare.json\tHIV-related cancer\t\tHIV-related cancer\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018628\t\t\tNCIT:C4046|Orphanet:443291|UMLS:C5680059|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1843219\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_clinical_situation|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|rare\n13012: MONDO:0018629\tbiolink:Disease\tfocal stiff limb syndrome\t\tGARD:0017756|MEDGEN:1672496|Orphanet:443804|UMLS:C4324606\tmondo-rare.json\tStiff leg syndrome|focal stiff-person syndrome\t\tStiff leg syndrome|focal stiff-person syndrome\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018629\t\t\tOrphanet:443804|UMLS:C4324606|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1672496\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_subtype_of_a_disorder|rare\n13013: MONDO:0018630\tbiolink:Disease\thereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer\tA cancer-predisposing condition characterized by the development of colorectal cancer not associated with colorectal polyposis, endometrial cancer, and various other cancers (such as malignant epithelial tumor of ovary, gastric, biliary tract, small bowel, and urinary tract cancer) that are frequently diagnosed at an early age.\tGARD:0025132|MEDGEN:232602|NCIT:C120083|OMIMPS:120435|Orphanet:443909|SCTID:315058005|UMLS:C1333990|icd11.foundation:8113015\tmondo-rare.json\tHNPCC|Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC)|colorectal cancer, hereditary nonpolyposis|familial nonpolyposis colon cancer|familial nonpolyposis colorectal cancer|hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer|hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer\t\tHNPCC|Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC)|colorectal cancer, hereditary nonpolyposis|familial nonpolyposis colon cancer|familial nonpolyposis colorectal cancer|hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer|hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018630\t\t\tNCIT:C120083|Orphanet:443909|UMLS:C1333990|http://id.who.int/icd/entity/8113015|http://identifiers.org/medgen/232602|http://identifiers.org/snomedct/315058005|https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS120435\tclingen|disease_grouping|gard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_group_of_disorders|otar|rare\n13014: MONDO:0018631\tbiolink:Disease\tMarie Unna hereditary hypotrichosis\tA rare autosomal dominant hair loss disorder characterized by the absence or scarcity of scalp hair, eyebrows, and eyelashes at birth; coarse and wiry hair during childhood; and progressive hair loss beginning around puberty.\tGARD:0003390|MEDGEN:419706|MESH:C535912|Orphanet:444|UMLS:C2931059\tmondo-rare.json\tHR hypotrichosis|MUHH|Marie Unna congenital hypotrichosis|hypotrichosis caused by mutation in HR|hypotrichosis, Marie Unna type\t\tHR hypotrichosis|MUHH|Marie Unna congenital hypotrichosis|hypotrichosis caused by mutation in HR|hypotrichosis, Marie Unna type\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018631\t\t\tOrphanet:444|UMLS:C2931059|http://identifiers.org/medgen/419706|http://identifiers.org/mesh/C535912\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|otar|rare\n13015: MONDO:0018632\tbiolink:Disease\t11q22.2q22.3 microdeletion syndrome\t\tGARD:0021858|MEDGEN:1805294|Orphanet:444002|UMLS:C5680062\tmondo-rare.json\t11q22.2-q22.3 deletion syndrome|Del(11)(q22.2q22.3)|monosomy 11q22.2-q22.3|monosomy 11q22.2q22.3\t\t11q22.2-q22.3 deletion syndrome|Del(11)(q22.2q22.3)|monosomy 11q22.2-q22.3|monosomy 11q22.2q22.3\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018632\t\t\tOrphanet:444002|UMLS:C5680062|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1805294\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|ordo_malformation_syndrome|orphanet_rare|rare\n13016: MONDO:0018633\tbiolink:Disease\t20q11.2 microdeletion syndrome\t20q11.2 microdeletion syndrome is a rare, genetic, syndromic intellectual disability characterized by psychomotor delay, hypotonia, feeding difficulties, failure to thrive, anomalies of the hands and feet (clinodactyly, camptodactyly, brachydactyly, feet malposition), and craniofacial dysmorphism. Associated prenatal growth retardation, and gastrointestinal, heart and eye anomalies have been reported.\tGARD:0021859|MEDGEN:1810637|Orphanet:444051|UMLS:C5680063\tmondo-rare.json\tDel(20)(q11.2)|monosomy 20q11\t\tDel(20)(q11.2)|monosomy 20q11\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018633\t\t\tOrphanet:444051|UMLS:C5680063|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1810637\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|ordo_malformation_syndrome|orphanet_rare|rare\n13017: MONDO:0018634\tbiolink:Disease\thereditary amyloidosis\tHereditary amyloidosis refers to a group of inherited conditions that make up one of the subtypes of amyloidosis. Hereditary amyloidosisis characterized by the deposit of an abnormal protein called amyloid in multiple organs of the body where it should not be, which causes disruption of organ tissue structure and function. In hereditary amyloidosis, amyloid deposits most often occur in tissues of the heart, kidneys, and nervous system. While symptoms of hereditary amyloidosis may appear in childhood, most individuals do not experience symptoms until adulthood. There are many types of hereditary amyloidosis associated with different gene mutations and abnormal proteins. The most common type of hereditary amyloidosis is transthyretin amyloidosis (ATTR),a condition in which the amyloid deposits are most often made up of the transthyretin protein which is made in the liver. Other examplesof hereditary amyloidosis include, but are not limited to, apolipoprotein AI amyloidosis (A ApoAI), gelsolin amyloidosis (A Gel), lysozyme amyloidosis (A Lys), cystatin C amyloidosis (A Cys), fibrinogen Aα-chain amyloidosis (A Fib), and apolipoprotein AII amyloidosis (A ApoAII). Most types of hereditary amyloidosis are inherited in an autosomal dominant manner. Treatment is focused on addressing symptoms of organ damage and slowing down the production of amyloid when possible through methods such as liver transplants.\tGARD:0006611|MEDGEN:148146|MESH:D028226|NCIT:C84555|Orphanet:444116|SCTID:367601000119103|UMLS:C0740340|icd11.foundation:1152878652\tmondo-rare.json\tamyloidosis hereditary|amyloidosis, Familial|familial amyloidosis|hereditary amyloidosis (disease)\t\tamyloidosis, Familial|hereditary amyloidosis (disease)\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018634\t\tamyloidosis hereditary|familial amyloidosis\tNCIT:C84555|Orphanet:444116|UMLS:C0740340|http://id.who.int/icd/entity/1152878652|http://identifiers.org/medgen/148146|http://identif... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n13018: MONDO:0018635\tbiolink:Disease\tidiopathic phalangeal acro-osteolysis\t\tGARD:0021860|MEDGEN:1803504|Orphanet:444316|UMLS:C5680064\tmondo-rare.json\tidiopathic phalangeal acroosteolysis\t\tidiopathic phalangeal acroosteolysis\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018635\t\t\tOrphanet:444316|UMLS:C5680064|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1803504\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|rare\n13019: MONDO:0018636\tbiolink:Disease\tautoimmune hemolytic anemia-autoimmune thrombocytopenia-primary immunodeficiency syndrome\t\tGARD:0017766|Orphanet:444463\tmondo-rare.json\tEvans syndrome associated with primary immunodeficiency|TPPII deficiency|TPPII-related immunodeficiency, autoimmunity, and neurodevelopmental delay with impaired glycolysis and lysosomal expansion disease|triangle disease|tripeptidyl-peptidase II deficiency\t\tEvans syndrome associated with primary immunodeficiency|TPPII deficiency|TPPII-related immunodeficiency, autoimmunity, and neurodevelopmental delay with impaired glycolysis and lysosomal expansion disease|triangle disease|tripeptidyl-peptidase II deficiency\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018636\t\t\tOrphanet:444463\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|rare\n13020: MONDO:0018637\tbiolink:Disease\tfamilial chylomicronemia syndrome\tA rare autosomal recessive disease characterized by the buildup in the blood of fat particles called chylomicrons (chylomicronemia), severe hypertriglyceridemia, and the risk of recurrent and potentially fatal pancreatitis and other complications. It is caused by mutations in the gene encoding LPL or, less frequently, by mutations in genes encoding other proteins necessary for LPL function.\tDOID:0111417|GARD:0006414|MEDGEN:1778100|Orphanet:444490|UMLS:C5442313\tmondo-rare.json\t\t\t\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018637\t\t\tDOID:0111417|Orphanet:444490|UMLS:C5442313|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1778100\tdoid_rare|gard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|otar|rare\n13021: MONDO:0018638\tbiolink:Disease\tpseudohypoaldosteronism\tAn inherited or acquired disorder of electrolyte metabolism, characterized by the inability of the renal tubules to respond to aldosterone. It is manifested by hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis, urinary salt wasting, normal or increased aldosterone secretion and normal glomerular filtration rate.\tDOID:4479|GARD:0021861|ICD9:255.8|MEDGEN:18721|MESH:D011546|NANDO:2100133|NANDO:2200367|NCIT:C85034|Orphanet:444916|SCTID:77098009|UMLS:C0033805\tmondo-rare.json\t\t\t\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018638\t\t\tDOID:4479|NCIT:C85034|Orphanet:444916|UMLS:C0033805|http://identifiers.org/medgen/18721|http://identifiers.org/mesh/D011546|http://identifiers.org/snomedct/77098009\tdisease_grouping|gard_rare|ncit_rare|ordo_group_of_disorders|otar|rare\n13022: MONDO:0018639\tbiolink:Disease\tcaudal regression-sirenomelia spectrum\tCaudal regression-sirenomelia spectrum is a group of rare genetic developmental defect during embryogenesis disorders characterized by varying degrees of caudal abdomen, pelvic, renal, anorectal, urogenital and/or lumbosacral spine malformations, with or without lower limb fusion. Phenotype is highly variable ranging from minor forms with isolated coccygeal agenesis to severe forms presenting with a single rudimentary limb. Central nervous system anomalies have also been reported.\tGARD:0021862|MEDGEN:1843168|Orphanet:444941|UMLS:C5681198\tmondo-rare.json\t\t\t\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018639\t\t\tOrphanet:444941|UMLS:C5681198|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1843168\tdisease_grouping|gard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_group_of_disorders|rare\n13023: MONDO:0018640\tbiolink:Disease\tsecondary vasculitis\t\tGARD:0021863|MEDGEN:1842960|Orphanet:445197|UMLS:C5681200\tmondo-rare.json\t\t\t\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018640\t\t\tOrphanet:445197|UMLS:C5681200|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1842960\tdisease_grouping|gard_rare|ordo_group_of_disorders|otar|rare\n13024: MONDO:0018642\tbiolink:Disease\tNIK deficiency\tA immunodeficiency disorder caused by loss of function mutation in NIK (MAP3K14).\tGARD:0021864|MEDGEN:1808868|Orphanet:447731|UMLS:C5680065\tmondo-rare.json\tMAP3K14 non-severe combined immunodeficiency|non-severe combined immunodeficiency caused by mutation in MAP3K14|primary immunodeficiency with multifaceted aberrant lymphoid immunity\t\tMAP3K14 non-severe combined immunodeficiency|non-severe combined immunodeficiency caused by mutation in MAP3K14|primary immunodeficiency with multifaceted aberrant lymphoid immunity\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018642\t\t\tOrphanet:447731|UMLS:C5680065|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1808868\tgard_rare|nord_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|otar|rare\n13025: MONDO:0018643\tbiolink:Disease\tsusceptibility to localized juvenile periodontitis\t\tGARD:0021865|MEDGEN:1812489|Orphanet:447740|UMLS:C5681199\tmondo-rare.json\t\t\t\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018643\t\t\tOrphanet:447740|UMLS:C5681199|http://identifiers.org/medgen/1812489\tgard_rare|ordo_disorder|orphanet_rare|predisposition|rare\n13026: MONDO:0018644\tbiolink:Disease\tautosomal dominant complex spastic paraplegia type 9B\t\tGARD:0021866|MEDGEN:1800402|Orphanet:447757|UMLS:C5568979\tmondo-rare.json\tAD-SPG9B\t\tAD-SPG9B\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0018644\t\t\tOrphanet:447757|UMLS:C5568979|